WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.610
Harry Kersh: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.080
we wanted to find out all the differences

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.310
between UK and US Starbucks.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:10.647
This is "Food Wars."

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.533
Now, hot coffees in the
UK come in four sizes.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.831
Firstly, we have a short coffee,

00:00:22.831 --> 00:00:23.910
which is 8 ounces.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.350
Joe Avella: The short, 8 ounces.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.720
Harry: Then we have a tall
coffee, which is 12 ounces.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.621
Joe: Next we have the tall, 12 ounces.

00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:32.950
Harry: Next is a grande coffee,

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.142
which comes in at 16 ounces.

00:00:35.142 --> 00:00:37.733
Joe: Then we got the grande, 16 ounces.

00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:40.650
Harry: A venti, or 20 ounces.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:41.483
Joe: Sensational.

00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:45.980
And the biggest one, the venti, 20 ounces.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.430
And in case you're wondering,
it's called the venti

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.480
because 20 in Italian is venti-twento.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.183
Harry: Just to double-check,

00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:58.820
we're gonna measure the
largest hot coffee in the UK.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.200
So, our venti coffee actually
came to closer to 18 ounces.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.497
Sort that out, Starbucks.

00:01:08.497 --> 00:01:09.713
Where's my 2 ounces?

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.220
Joe: Now I'm going to
measure the venti coffee,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.016
which should be 20
ounces minus a few sips.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:21.743
Wait.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.056
Came prepared.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.500
Whoa, come on! I did not drink that much.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.373
Oh, Starbucks.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.470
That's not...no, that's not even two.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.210
That's, like, an ounce.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.563
Harry: Cold coffees in the
UK come in three sizes.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:42.681
A tall.

00:01:42.681 --> 00:01:45.670
Joe: The tall, 12 ounces.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:46.503
Harry: Grande.

00:01:46.503 --> 00:01:48.920
Joe: We got the grande.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:49.780
Harry: And venti.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.340
Joe: Here's where things
get kind of weird.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.220
Our venti iced is 24 ounces.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.110
And we have another
size up, trenti, right?

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.853
Trenta, and that is 30 ounces.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.203
Harry: So, a UK cold venti
is exactly 20 ounces.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.100
Joe: Yeah, 21.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.950
I thought this was supposed to be 24.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.165
"How many fluid ounces

00:02:23.165 --> 00:02:27.490
venti iced"?

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.070
24.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.521
How much is it, 21?

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.593
Starbucks, with the ice,
you still don't have enough.

00:02:38.577 --> 00:02:40.650
Aah. I'm taking a sip
of every single drink.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.640
I'm taking a sip of every single drink!

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:44.748
Let's do this!

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.870
Harry: Let's compare some
seemingly identical drinks

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.060
to see how much they
match up under the hood.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.570
So, a venti latte in the UK

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.295
contains 235 calories,

00:02:56.295 --> 00:02:57.720
7.9 grams of fat,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.770
21.1 grams of sugar,

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.257
and 225 milligrams of caffeine.

00:03:02.257 --> 00:03:03.590
Joe: And in the US,

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.990
the same venti latte has 250 calories,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.748
and that's 9 grams of fat

00:03:08.748 --> 00:03:11.920
and 150 milligrams of caffeine.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.096
And you know I'm taking a sip.

00:03:14.096 --> 00:03:15.721
Mm!

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.758
Kind of cold. It's been
sitting out for hours.

00:03:19.758 --> 00:03:23.300
So our latte's got more sugar,
more calories, and more fat.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.770
"The trinity of deliciousness"
is what I call it.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.270
Harry: Let's try something
a little heavier.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.780
If you were to order a
venti caramel Frappuccino

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.210
from a UK Starbucks,

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.893
it would contain 426 calories,

00:03:33.893 --> 00:03:35.992
61.1 grams of sugar,

00:03:35.992 --> 00:03:38.610
and 117 milligrams of caffeine.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.590
Joe: Harry, your Frappuccino's

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:40.750
looking a little sad there, buddy.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:42.140
It looks like all your
whipped cream melted,

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.243
so here's a pro tip from the USA.

00:03:45.911 --> 00:03:47.310
Mm.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.976
470 calories,

00:03:48.976 --> 00:03:51.909
74 carbs, 73 of which are sugar,

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:55.631
and 120 milligrams of caffeine.

00:03:55.631 --> 00:03:56.606
Mm.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.090
Damn. Oh, yuck. Yuck!

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.840
That's what that tastes like?

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:02.940
Harry: Now, it's
important to remember that

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.900
for cold drinks like Frappuccinos,

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.330
the US venti is actually
4 fluid ounces larger

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.450
than the UK's.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:09.750
If you were to do the math to work out

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.320
the nutrition per fluid ounce,

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.790
the UK actually beats the US
in virtually every metric.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.130
Let's see if we can
get any clues as to why

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.180
from the ingredients.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.490
Now, in the UK, a
cappuccino from Starbucks

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.890
contains just two ingredients,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.970
milk and brewed espresso.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.930
Joe: And in the US, our
cappuccinos are made

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.900
from milk and brewed espresso.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.488
Look at that. Same thing, finally.

00:04:32.488 --> 00:04:34.780
Mm. Mm!

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:35.646
Harry: Now, for the most part,

00:04:35.646 --> 00:04:37.179
the hot coffees in both countries

00:04:37.179 --> 00:04:39.600
are just those two ingredients
in different quantities.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.000
Things get a little bit more complicated

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.590
once you start to add
things like syrups, however.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:44.660
For example, in the UK,

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.670
if you add caramel syrup
to your cappuccino,

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.170
that adds the following ingredients:

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.840
sugar, water, natural flavoring,

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.420
concentrate of apple, carrot, hibiscus,

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.515
molasses, citric acid,
and potassium sorbate.

00:04:58.845 --> 00:04:59.678
Joe: Aah.

00:04:59.678 --> 00:05:01.444
Whereas our caramel syrup has sugar,

00:05:01.444 --> 00:05:04.575
corn syrup, butter (cream (milk) salt),

00:05:04.575 --> 00:05:07.013
uh, water, heavy cream,

00:05:07.013 --> 00:05:09.600
nonfat dry milk,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.740
natural flavors, salt,

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.365
mono diggly-psoriasis?

00:05:13.365 --> 00:05:15.123
I don't know what that word is.

00:05:15.123 --> 00:05:17.540
Soy lecithin and sulfites.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:20.730
The point is, our caramel
syrup has actual caramel in it.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.170
How do you like that?

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.290
Harry: Now let's try something
with a lot more ingredients.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.750
This is a java chip
Frappuccino from the UK,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.570
and it contains the following.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:30.403
Both: Ice.

00:05:30.403 --> 00:05:31.373
Milk.

00:05:31.373 --> 00:05:32.640
Coffee Frappuccino syrup,

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.105
Harry: Which is water --
Joe: Which is sugar, water --

00:05:34.105 --> 00:05:37.438
[both reciting different ingredients]

00:05:37.438 --> 00:05:41.761
[fast-forwarding]

00:05:41.761 --> 00:05:42.997
Harry: Sodium bicarbonate
Joe: Sodium

00:05:42.997 --> 00:05:43.830
Harry: and a mocha drizzle,
Joe: bi-cow-bonate,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:44.663
Harry: which is water,

00:05:44.663 --> 00:05:45.727
Harry: mocha powder, sugar,
Joe: Soy lecithin,

00:05:45.727 --> 00:05:46.560
Harry: cocoa processed with
Joe: natural flavors,

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.410
Harry: alkaline, and vanillin.
Joe: oh, and salt.

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:51.870
Ladies and gentlemen, it
is a "Food Wars" first,

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.720
the first time something in the UK

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.610
has an ingredients list longer
than something in the US.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.545
Time to celebrate, baby!

00:05:58.545 --> 00:06:01.628
[party horn blowing]

00:06:05.274 --> 00:06:06.890
Harry: Here is everything
you can only find on the menu

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.530
at a UK Starbucks.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.980
Joe: And this is everything
you can get in a US Starbucks.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:13.530
Harry: Now, we'll start
with a quick disclaimer.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.020
One of the big selling points of Starbucks

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:16.390
is that you can heavily customize

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:17.870
pretty much everything on the menu.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.467
To save ourselves doing
a million different

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.100
combinations of exclusive items,

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.370
what we're gonna do is focus mostly

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:24.260
on things that are on
the menu as they are,

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.740
with just a few combinations
of syrups and toppings

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.120
that you can get in one or the other.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.050
Firstly, we have this cute little cortado.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.360
Secondly, we have a cold foam cappuccino,

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.583
which is like an iced cappuccino

00:06:34.583 --> 00:06:36.840
with just some foam on top of it.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.740
Then we have some temporary
season exclusives.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:39.990
So, at the moment, you can get a

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.620
peanut butter cup Frappuccino in the UK,

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.670
as well as a chocolate
s'mores Frappuccino,

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:46.840
which sounds pretty damn good.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.300
Joe: In the US, along with the blond roast

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.170
you can get in the UK, we
also have a dark roast,

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.739
and we have something
called the Pike Place roast.

00:06:52.739 --> 00:06:55.190
And that roast is a tribute
to the first Starbucks

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:58.120
that's near the Pike Place
Market in Seattle, Washington.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.470
I've been to that Starbucks.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:00.910
It's fine.

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:04.103
Exclusive to the US, we have
the caramel cloud macchiato.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:08.650
Ooh, I do not like that.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:12.613
The mocha cookie crumble Frappuccino.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:15.653
Woof.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.230
The caramel ribbon crunch Frappuccino.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.000
I mean, the regular caramel one was gross.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.263
Let's see how this one is.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:25.070
Oh, God, it's worse.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.670
Oh, it's so much worse.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.345
And we also have this,

00:07:28.345 --> 00:07:31.036
a vanilla sweet cream cold brew.

00:07:31.036 --> 00:07:32.751
The ice has melted.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.670
Woof.

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:42.270
I bet that was good six
hours ago when it was made.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.820
Harry: And what we've done is combined

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:46.290
as many UK exclusives as
we can into one drink,

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.820
which I'm calling the UK Only.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:50.850
We've got some almond,
some sugar-free caramel,

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.000
some sugar-free hazelnut, and some mango,

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.850
which is gonna be an interesting mix.

00:07:59.795 --> 00:08:01.286
Hoo!

00:08:01.286 --> 00:08:02.370
That is sweet.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.280
[laughs]

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.840
Joe: And of course at
the Starbucks in the US,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.830
we have our own unique customizations.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.060
For instance, pineapple ginger syrup,

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.520
raspberry syrup, toffee nut syrup,

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.750
a cascara topping,
cinnamon dolce sprinkles.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:19.990
We have chocolate, cinnamon,
nutmeg, and vanilla powders

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:22.530
and a whole range of cold foams.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.713
So, of course, I went ahead

00:08:24.713 --> 00:08:27.254
and had all those things
put into one drink,

00:08:27.254 --> 00:08:29.653
which I am calling the USA Only.

00:08:35.209 --> 00:08:36.042
Mm-mm.

00:08:38.614 --> 00:08:39.447
Mm-mm.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.343
Oh, man, that's weird.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.913
Nothing in that works together.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.124
Mm.

00:08:50.124 --> 00:08:52.841
[ambient music]

00:08:54.525 --> 00:08:57.030
[echoes] That caffeine's
starting to hit, baby.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:00.784
It's starting to hit!

00:09:03.957 --> 00:09:05.295
[party horn blowing]

00:09:05.295 --> 00:09:08.386
[ambient music continues]

00:09:14.995 --> 00:09:17.380
[tapping table]

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:19.580
Starbucks. [laughs]

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.180
Harry: Now, this series
isn't called "Drink Wars,"

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.510
and thankfully Starbucks
has lots of food items.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:24.590
Here are some things you'll only find

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.393
on the menu at a UK Starbucks.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:30.700
Let's start with the bakery section.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:32.350
Here in the UK, on the bakery menu,

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:35.450
you will find a chocolate
chunk shortbread cookie.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.470
You will find this delightful

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:39.070
little gingerbread barista man.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.620
You will find this pain aux raisins.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:44.670
We also have some luxury fruit toast,

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.240
which comes with these
little cute pots of jam.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.390
And then finally we've
got a cinnamon swirl,

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:51.400
which is kind of just a cinnamon roll.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.370
Our breakfast options
are slightly limited.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.210
Most of what we have is a
range of breakfast rolls,

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:58.090
which is just kind of like a
breakfast sandwich in the UK,

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.040
but served on crusty white bread.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:03.390
Some options include a bacon sandwich.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.110
Bacon sandwiches are a
pretty big deal over here.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.270
[slo-mo] I like mine with ketchup.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:09.440
And then also, if you want
something a little bit more,

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.340
you can go for an all-day
breakfast sandwich,

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.100
which is bacon, sausage,
and egg all in one roll.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.910
Joe: Here are all the
US-exclusive food items.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:22.160
So, a lot of different breakfast
sandwiches for Starbucks.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.310
This one right here is

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.920
the Impossible meat sandwich.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:31.200
Spinach, egg, and feta cheese wrap.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.810
This is my go-to one. Love it.

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:34.670
These egg bites, these little, um,

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:35.830
gonna definitely pronounce this wrong,

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:39.230
these little sood-vee egg wraps. Sood vee?

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:40.615
I'll be straight up, I've
never had one of these.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.103
Holy s---, these are so good.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:50.500
Oh, my God!

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.870
A lot of baked goods, like
this little friendly guy,

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.380
just in time for the holiday season,

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.350
a little snowman cake pop.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:57.790
Bunch of different
varieties of sugar cookies.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:59.880
I got this one 'cause I
think it looks the nicest,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.670
but I don't want this at all.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:03.310
Oh, but you know what I do love?

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:06.470
Dude. Danishes, especially
cheese danishes.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:08.700
We also got scones.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.600
So I'm going with the blueberry scone.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:12.933
Can we get Harry's face on there?

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:15.315
Scone Harry?

00:11:15.315 --> 00:11:16.450
Harry: Sorry to break it to you, Joe,

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:17.960
but that is not a scone.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:19.100
Few things to point out.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.590
Firstly, it's pronounced skahn, not skohn.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:22.477
Secondly, they should all --

00:11:22.477 --> 00:11:25.343
Joe: The marshmallow dream bar,

00:11:25.343 --> 00:11:28.660
aka a rice crispy treat.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:30.970
What are we doing?

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.840
We're going to Starbucks in the morning,

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:33.740
and we're getting cookies

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:35.960
and rice crispy treats for breakfast?

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:37.543
No, no.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:42.970
They got bagels.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.100
I asked for cream cheese, and they forgot.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:47.930
Pumpkin cream cheese muffin.

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:49.020
It's got pumpkin,

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.140
a little glob of cream
cheese in it right there.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.280
Muffins are OK in the morning.

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:53.810
I'm pro morning muffin.

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:56.570
Last and probably least
because I've never had one,

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:58.530
nor have I ever wanted one,

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.100
Starbucks in the US have a lot

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.990
of what they call protein boxes.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.630
Chicken and quinoa.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:06.220
Looks like a salad.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:07.910
Harry: You guys have pretty
much all the lunch options

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.335
that we have, in terms of, like,

00:12:09.335 --> 00:12:11.760
paninis, sandwiches, and wraps.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.820
One thing I did wanna highlight was this,

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:17.340
which is a cheese and
Marmite mini ciabatta.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.850
So, Marmite, as some of you will know,

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:21.390
is a spread that's
quite popular in the UK.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:23.740
It's like the byproduct of making beer.

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:25.645
When the yeast ferments,
it kind of leaves, like,

00:12:25.645 --> 00:12:27.280
a sludge on the bottom of the tank.

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.592
And that is Marmite.

00:12:28.592 --> 00:12:31.420
But it somehow tastes really good.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.370
Joe: Harry, no to Marmite.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.580
It's bad, stop it.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:37.530
Harry: So, on top of classic
English breakfast tea,

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:39.980
we also have some
exclusive teas to the UK.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:42.710
So, we'll start with the
jasmine pearl green tea,

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:45.150
which is just our exclusive
version of the green tea.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.690
Here we've got something
called a youthberry tea,

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:49.480
which apparently helps keep you young.

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:50.940
Here is some chamomile tea,

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:51.962
which is great if you just want something

00:12:51.962 --> 00:12:53.370
to relax before bed.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:54.960
Then we've got some frozen teas as well.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.580
So, this is a frozen iced
tea made with apple and kiwi.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.290
This is a frozen iced
tea with tropical mango.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:04.070
And then this is a frozen
strawberry and lime tea.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:06.440
Joe: Here in the US, we have
a lot of exclusive teas too.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.740
These are those exclusive teas.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.920
For hot ones, we have a jade citrus mint,

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:14.090
a peach tranquility tea.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:15.060
Think they missed a trick there,

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.700
not calling it the peach tranquili-tea.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:18.533
[audience cheering]

00:13:18.533 --> 00:13:21.100
Conner Blake came up with that, not me.

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.550
The revved up tea right there.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:24.616
And then for the cold ones,

00:13:24.616 --> 00:13:26.960
we got the royal English breakfast tea.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:28.396
Why don't you guys have the

00:13:28.396 --> 00:13:31.255
iced royal English breakfast tea?

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:34.693
A matcha lemonade tea,

00:13:34.693 --> 00:13:36.943
a iced guava black tea,

00:13:36.943 --> 00:13:40.751
and the passion tango iced tea.

00:13:40.751 --> 00:13:43.526
Mm, mm, mm!

